  "description": "The break displaces and the failure swing is there, but price never closes beyond the break candle's low. Five closes later the candidate expires and the trend comes back.",
  "labelling_rationale": "The first 52 hand-written candles are one shared run-up, and nothing in them is the subject of this case -- they exist so §3.6 has a trend to act on. Eight external pivots print at 5-candle spacing, each strictly beyond the five candles on either side, so §3.1 confirms each of them exactly 5 closes after its pivot: a low at 87 (SEED), a high at 113 (SEED), a low at 93 (HL), a high at 125 (HH), a low at 105 (HL), a high at 137 (HH), a low at 117 (HL), and finally a high at 131 -- which is an **LH**, because 131 is below the 137 before it. §3.4 turns the trend BULLISH when the last two highs read HH, HH and the last two lows read HL, HL, which happens the moment the 137 high confirms. The LH that follows does not disturb that: §3.4's entry rule is only consulted while the state is RANGING, and a trending market printing an occasional lower high has not stopped trending. That LH is the second thing the run-up exists for -- it is §3.6's **failure swing**, origin 2(b). **The CHoCH.** §3.6 makes a CHoCH the first close below the most recent confirmed swing low that initiated the current up-leg -- the latest confirmed HL. That is the low at 117, confirmed five closes after its pivot and never consumed. The break candle opens at 120 and closes at 108, which is below 117, so the CHoCH fires and §3.6's stated effect follows: the trend moves to BULLISH_CAUTION, not to BEARISH. §3.6 separates the two deliberately -- it calls that separation \"the doctrine's protection against reversal-chasing\" -- and the payload records both sides of the move, previous_trend BULLISH and next_trend BULLISH_CAUTION. The swing is marked consumed, so the same 117 low cannot produce a second CHoCH on a later close. External swings are preferred over internal ones: §3.6 admits an internal HL only \"if no external HL exists in the current leg\", and here one does, so swing_strength is EXTERNAL. **Why no MSS.** This case is the mirror of `s4-a-choch-without-displacement-is-not-an-mss`: the two conditions that were missing there hold here, and the one that held there is missing. The break candle is the same 120-to-108 bar, so displacement is satisfied on all three of §5.10's tests, and the 131 LH is still the failure swing. What never arrives is follow-through. §3.6 requires \"a subsequent candle closes beyond the CHoCH break candle's extreme in the break direction within 5 closed candles\"; that extreme is the break candle's low of 107.5, and the five candles after it close at 110.25, 111.25, 112.25, 113.25 and 114.25 -- every one of them above it, because price drifted back up instead of continuing. The candidate therefore expires on the fifth close, and §3.6 says exactly what happens then: \"the CHoCH remains a recorded warning, the trend returns from CAUTION to its prior state, and the MSS candidate is recorded failed\". The CHoCH event stays in the record, failed_candidates reads 1, and the trend is BULLISH again. Nothing is deleted, which is the point -- a warning that did not develop is still a fact about the market. Every run-up candle is the same shape -- a 0.5 body inside a 2.0 range, centred on its level -- so its high and low both track that level strictly and the swing tests are decided by the level alone. It also fixes the mean 20-candle body at 0.5, which is what makes §5.10's `body >= 2.0 x mean_body_20` test a number a labeller can evaluate rather than an average of noise.",
  "labelled_by": "drafted by Claude Opus 5 2026-09-12 from SLS §3.6; pending developer verification",
